 lang="source_other source_other">Iṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">I</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">ndān·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">ṅdan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">tuli<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨s⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>y<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿y?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>s<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">s</search></split>·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">tuliy·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">In other inscriptions we find <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tulis</span><!--space--> in this context.</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">luĪranya</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">lviranya</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">sapaṁhīṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">saṁ pahī</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">The position of he first <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">anusvāra</span><!--space--> is ambiguous, standing midway between the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sa</span><!--space--> and the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pa</span>; evidently, it has been engraved unusually far to the left to avoid the descender from the preceding line.</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">sakaL̥viḥnya</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">saṅka L̥viḥnya</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">sodhāra</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">sovāra</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r7⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">a<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>dh<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>ikāna</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">avikāna</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r8⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">haji <!--space--><span class="abbr" tip="Abbreviated text" lang="source_other source_other">yu</span><!--space--> <span class="num" lang="source_other source_other">1</span><!--space--> rakryān·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">One expects <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">haji yu 1 I rakryān·</span>. The expected preposition is absent also in the following entries in the list.</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r8⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">ra<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨k⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>g<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿g?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>k<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">k</search></split>ryān·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r9⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">pūdā</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pu Udā</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r9⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">pu tuna<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text (&lt;span class=&quot;italics&quot;&gt;eccentric_ductus&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>ṁ<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pu tunaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">This is the expected reading (compare with <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKSobhamerta" lang="eng latin">Sobhamerta</link><!--space--> 4v6). The <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">anusvāra</span><!--space--> is irregularly shaped.</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">paku bāṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pakuban</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">iṁ pakaraṇān·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">i pakaraṅān·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r14⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">vadvā</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">vadna</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r15⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">I tiruAn· sumuḍan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">I tiruAn· samuḍan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">There is no need to restore <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">I tiruAn· saṁ sumuḍan·</span><!--space--> following the reissued <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKSobhamerta" lang="eng latin">Sobhamerta</link><!--space--> charter (5v1), as the original stone inscriptions of Sindok’s reign read <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tiruAn sumuḍan</span>.</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r15⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">brahmaśiva</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">brāḥma śiva</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r16⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">saṁ pakudān·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">sapakudan</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r17⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">paskaran·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pasturan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r18⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">pu bukəṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pu bukə</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r18⟩</span><!--space--> <span><span class="abbr" tip="Abbreviated text" lang="source_other source_other">mā</span><!--space--> <span class="num" lang="source_other source_other">10</span></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading"><span class="abbr" tip="Abbreviated text" lang="source_other source_other">mā</span><!--space--> <span class="num" lang="source_other source_other">1</span></span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r1⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Atəhə<span class="supplied" tip="Lost text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>r<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>pat<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><span class="supplied" tip="Lost text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>i<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span>ḥ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">Atəhə<span class="supplied" tip="Lost text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>r a<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span>taḥ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">What remains visible of the damaged <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">akṣara</span><!--space--> assumed by Wibowo to be <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ra</span><!--space--> does not seem to be compatible with that reading, and a more convincing restitution is furnished by line 3.4, where we read <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patiḥ maṁharap· babak·</span>.</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r2⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">ghoṣa Amasaṅakan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">yoṣa Apasaṅakan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r3⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">virikan· vḍihan hlai</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">virikan· hlai</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r5⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">hujuṁ, juṁhur·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">hujan juhur·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">bhanū, bakaṭəl·, ḍaluṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">bhanū, ḍaluṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r7⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Urak·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">rek·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r7⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">bandhu</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">bhandu</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r9⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">dr̥vyak·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">dravyak·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">Cf. <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">guru dr̥vyak·</span><!--space--> in 1.5.</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">kinabehanya</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">kibanehanya</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">śikha</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">śila</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">dhane<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨ṣ⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>m<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿m?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>ṣ<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">ṣ</search></split>ṭi</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">dhanemvi</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">rāma ri lmaḥ tulis· milu, …, bandī vineḥ</span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">It seems that <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">milu</span><!--space--> has been misplaced, and need to be moved to stand before <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vinaiḥ</span>.</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">pəgeṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pəgaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r11⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">vudyaṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">vunyaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">Cf. <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKKinawe" lang="eng latin">Kinawe</link><!--space--> line 12 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">samgat laṇḍayan pu vudyaṅ</span>.</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r11⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">tuha kalaṁ karu<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text (&lt;span class=&quot;italics&quot;&gt;eccentric_ductus&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>k<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>·</span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">One could also read <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">karukh·</span>, but <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">karuk·</span><!--space--> is expected. Cf. <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKBimalasrama" lang="eng latin">Bimalasrama</link><!--space--> 10r3 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṁ karuk·</span>.</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r12⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">kalaṁ manaḥ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">talimahaḥ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r12⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">vi<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>la<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">vdiha</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">Perhaps the scribe intended and wrote <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vilaṁ</span><!--space--> (cf. <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vilaṁ vanua</span><!--space--> in 1r11)?</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">balun·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">balur·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">lumakvānusuk·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">lumakva Anusuk·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">saṅkap· riṁ saji</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">saṅkap saji</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">lumakvānusu<nline break="false" phantom="false" significant="false"><span tip="Line start" lang="zxx latin">⟨3r14⟩</span></nline>k·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">lumakva Anusuk·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r14⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">harava <!--space--><span class="abbr" tip="Abbreviated text" lang="source_other source_other">mā</span><!--space--> <span class="num" lang="source_other source_other">10</span></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">harava <!--space--><span class="abbr" tip="Abbreviated text" lang="source_other source_other">mā</span><!--space--> <span class="num" lang="source_other source_other">1</span>-</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r14⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">vitāna</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">vitāna,</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">Wibowo reads a punctuation sign after <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vitāna</span>, but I cannot see it on the photo of the plate, which admittedly is a bit worn here. Its presence is possible, but not necessary; some contemporary inscriptions punctuate the text here in this formula, while others do not.</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r17⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Īndaḥ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">Indaḥ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r18⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">yajamānākāśa</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">yajamānā Akāśa</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r18⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">pretāsura</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">preta Asura</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r19⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">nandīśvara</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">naṇḍīśvara</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r19⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other"><split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨ṣ⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>m<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿m?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>ṣ<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">ṣ</search></split>advināyaka</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">ṣad vināyaka</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r19⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">kālamr̥tyu</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">kālamratyu</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r1⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">sākṣībhūta</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">sāṣībhūta</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r2⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Ikaṁṅ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">Ikeṁṅ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r3⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">gr̥hastha</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">gr̥hasta</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r4⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">baba<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨k⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>t<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿t?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>k<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">k</search></split>a</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">babata</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin"><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Trigangga2003_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Trigangga 2003</span></link>, p. 23, n. 41</span><!--space--> (<link href="/texts/INSIDENKLinggasuntan" lang="eng latin">Linggasuntan</link><!--space--> face C, line 32) proposed to correct to <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">babakan</span>, but <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">babaka</span><!--space--> is found in <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKAnjukladang" lang="eng latin">Anjuk Ladang</link><!--space--> back, line 14; in <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKParadah2" lang="eng latin">Paradah II</link>, face C line 79; <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKKampak" lang="eng latin">Kampak</link><!--space--> back, line 10.</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r5⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">tṅanan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">tuganan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r5⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">vkassakan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">vkasan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r6⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">pamuṅvan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pamaṅvan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r8⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">jalamm er·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">jalamm ər·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">See the correction <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dalam er</span><!--space--> proposed in <!--space--><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Trigangga2003_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Trigangga 2003</span></link>, p. 23, n. 43</span>. The same spelling is found in <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKLinggasuntan" lang="eng latin">Linggasuntan</link><!--space--> face C, line 39 and <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKParadah2" lang="eng latin">Paradah II</link><!--space--> (back line 34), <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKAnjukladang" lang="eng latin">Anjuk Ladang</link><!--space--> back, line 19, and <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKRukam" lang="eng latin">Rukam</link><!--space--> 2r18. On the other hand, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dalamm er·</span><!--space--> is found in <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKParadah1" lang="eng latin">Paradah I</link><!--space--> (2B13). The form <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dalam air</span><!--space--> is found in <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKGilikan" lang="eng latin">Gilikan</link><!--space--> (first plate, v11).</span><!--space--> — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r8⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">ṅkānan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">ṅkā</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r9⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Ika naraka</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">Iṁ kanaraka</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r9⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">duḥ<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨kh⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>l<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿l?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>kh<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">kh</search></split>a</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">duḥla</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">hiḍəpənya</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">hiḍəpəAnya</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">dadyananya</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">dayananya</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r10⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">maṅkanātmahananikaṁṅ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">maṅkanā tmhana nikaṁṅ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r12⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">kaniṣṭ<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>h<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>a-</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">kaniṣṭa-</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">saṅasaṅān·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">saṅsaṅān·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">ḍeṁ hañaṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">ḍe hañaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">ḍeṁ hasin·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">ḍe hasin·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r13⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Ina<nline break="false" phantom="false" significant="false"><span tip="Line start" lang="zxx latin">⟨4r14⟩</span></nline>ri<span class="supplied" tip="Lost text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>ṁ<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> suṇḍa</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">Ina<nline break="false" phantom="false" significant="false"><span tip="Line start" lang="zxx latin">⟨4r14⟩</span></nline>risuṇḍa</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r14⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">p<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨ə:⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>i:<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿i:?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>ə:<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">ə:</search></split>han·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">pīhan·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r14⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">saṅa bheda</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ abheda</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r14⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">vahuta <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Omitted text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>rāma<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span><!--space--> Ika<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>ṁ<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta Ikaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r15⟩</span><!--space--> <span><split><display><span class="orig" tip="Non-standard text (standardisation: &lt;span class=&quot;reg&quot;&gt;⟨celeṁ⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¡</display>coloṁ<display lang="study_other latin">!</display></span><span class="reg" tip="Standardised text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;orig&quot;&gt;&lt;display xml:lang=&quot;study_other latin&quot;&gt;¡&lt;/display&gt;coloṁ&lt;display xml:lang=&quot;study_other latin&quot;&gt;!&lt;/display&gt;&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>celeṁ<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">celeṁ</search></split></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">celeṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r15⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">sovaṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">sovaṁ-sovaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r15⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">kuravu ku<nline break="false" phantom="false" significant="false"><span tip="Line start" lang="zxx latin">⟨4r16⟩</span></nline>ri<span class="unclear" tip="Unclear text" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>ma,<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span></span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">kura vuku<nline break="false" phantom="false" significant="false"><span tip="Line start" lang="zxx latin">⟨4r16⟩</span></nline>riḥ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r18⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">Atəh<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨ə⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>i<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿i?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>ə<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">ə</search></split><span class="space" tip="Space left blank in a line because (part of) another character hanging down from the previous line encroaches on the current line" lang="source_other source_other">⊔</span>r·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">Atəhər·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r18⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">paṅin-aṅan·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">paṅinaṅin·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span><!--space--> • <!--space--><span lang="eng latin">Or should we consider that this is the same as the term encountered in 1.12 and edit <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅin-aṅ<split><display><span class="sic" tip="Incorrect text (emendation: &lt;span class=&quot;corr&quot;&gt;⟨i⟩&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">¿</display>a<display lang="study_other latin">?</display></span><span class="corr" tip="Emended text (original: &lt;span class=&quot;sic&quot;&gt;¿a?&lt;/span&gt;)" lang="source_other source_other"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display>i<display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span></display><search lang="source_other source_other">i</search></split>n·</span>?</span></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r19⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">manaṅgap· ta rakryān·</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">manaṅgap· taṁ rakryān·</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>. — <!--space--><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r19⟩</span><!--space--> <span lang="source_other source_other">kramanikaṁ</span><!--space--> ⬦ <!--space--><span class="reading" tip="Reading" lang="source_other source_other">kramanikanaṁ</span><!--space--> <span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">W</span></link></span>.</para></apparatus><translation><head lang="eng latin">Translation</head><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨1r1⟩</span><!--space--> Homage be to the six Vināyakas!</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨1r1–1r4⟩</span><!--space--> Hail! Elapsed Śaka year 861, month of Bhadrapada, fifth <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tithi</span><!--space--> of the waning fortnight, Vās, Pahiṅ, Friday, lunar mansion Aśvinī, deity Aśvī, conjunction Viṣkambha. That was the time that the decree of the Great King, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakai</span><!--space--> Halu, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Siṇḍok Śrī Īśānavikrama, received by the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> minister of Halu, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Sahasra, descended to the official of Kanuruhan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Udā, instructing with regard to the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">varuk</span><!--space--> land<note n="1"><para lang="eng latin">The term <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">varuk</span><!--space--> is not well understood. <!--space--><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.v. varuk</span><!--space--> glosses “<span lang="eng latin">a piece of ground (acquired in a part. way? bought?)</span>”, while <!--space--><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Wibowo 1979</span></link>, p. 13</span><!--space--> tentatively suggested: “<span lang="eng latin">Mungkin kata <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">lmaḥ waruk</span><!--space--> menunjuk bagian yang masih hutan belukar</span>”.</para></note><!--space--> at Ālasantan, district of Bavaṅ Mapapan, that it be made the freehold of <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>or: that it be made into a freehold by<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān kabayān</span>, mother of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> minister of Halu, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Sahasra. Its total: 13 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tampah</span>, apart from the housing-premises <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> orchards, the price for them being 12 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kāṭi</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of gold<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>. The headmen of Ālasantan together with the inhabitants of the village sold the land.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨1r4–1r8⟩</span><!--space--> Enumeration of the headmen receiving gold:<note n="2"><para lang="eng latin">The headword of each entry in the list (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vinkas</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">apkan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">gusti</span>, etc.) is directly followed by a name, further names separated by median dots.</para></note></para><dlist><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vinəkas</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">the elder Vantil, Pastī, Sukhamī, the teacher Dr̥vyak, Kaḍaṅayan, Suji, Rambay, Jikən, Lubhī</para></value><key><para lang="eng latin">market seller</para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Maṇḍarah</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">gusti</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Aṅkat</para></value><key><para lang="eng latin">scribes</para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Tambi, Bhr̥tyah, Bharalī, Duvəg, Baryuṅ, Tuṅgəṅ, Vulakan, Popoṅ</para></value><key><para lang="eng latin">herald</para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Mañciṅ</para></value><key><para lang="eng latin">foresters</para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Varakas, Kabat, Bahu, Khyāti, Suraṅgaṇā, Telā, Puluh, Bhujī, Saṇḍak</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vakulan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Mandon</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṇḍal</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Lālaṅ</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">akajar</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Kumīs, Ḍampal, Kuhal, Capil</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">variga</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Kālih Bakacak, Busta, Saṅlā</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuha vərəh</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">Dayī, Cabil</para></value></dlist><para lang="eng latin">Such was the total of the headmen receiving gold.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨1r8–1r14⟩</span><!--space--> The purpose of the land being made a freehold was to become a <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> freehold<note n="3"><para lang="eng latin">The compound term <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> is reminiscent of the much more common pattern <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma ka-...-an</span><!--space--> (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma kajurugusalyan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma kavajvan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma kakaṭikan</span>, etc.) seen in numerous Old Javanese inscriptions. But in the present context the word <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> is derived with <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pa-...-an</span><!--space--> circumfix, from <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kurumbigi</span><!--space--> which seems to be an alternate of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kurumbhāgi</span>, “<span lang="eng latin">a kind of iron tool (knife)</span>” (<span><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link></span>).</para></note><!--space--> of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān kabayān</span>. Its status would be independent. It should not be entered as before by the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>s, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s, the headmen, and all types of collectors of royal revenues: various <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">miśra</span>s, various types of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vulu</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅuraṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kriṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paḍam</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">manimpiki</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paraṇakan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">limus galuh</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅaruhan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">taji</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vatu tajam</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sukun</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">halu varak</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakadut</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pinilai</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kataṅgaran</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tapa haji</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">air haji</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">malandaṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ləca</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ləblab</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kalaṅkaṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kutak</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">taṅkil</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tr̥pan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">salvīt</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vatu valaṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pamaṇikan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sikpān</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rumbān</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tiṅkəs</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">viji kavaḥ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vilaṅ vanua</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuha dagaṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">juru guśali</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuhān nambi</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuhān huñjaman</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">juru juḍi</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">juru jalir</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">miśra hino</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vli hapū</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vli vaduṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vli tambaṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vli pañjut</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vli harəṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pabisar</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">palamak</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pakaluṅkuṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">urutan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dampulan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tpuṅ kavuṅ</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suṅsuṅ paṅuraṅ</span>, forest dwellers (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pasuk alas</span>), <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">payuṅan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pāṅin-aṅin</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sipad bilut</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">puluṅ paḍi</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">skar tahun</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">panrāṅan</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">panusuh</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hopan</span>, mahouts (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pahaliman</span>), eunuchs (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kḍi</span>), healers (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">valyan</span>), actors (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vidu</span>), <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sambal</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sumbul</span>, royal servants (<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hulun haji</span>), <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pamr̥ṣi</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sinaguha</span>, the courtiers <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vatak i jro</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, etc., shall not enter into the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> freehold of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">varuk</span><!--space--> land at Ālasantan. Only the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> founder of the freehold herself shall have authority over all its royal revenues.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨1r14–1r16⟩</span><!--space--> Likewise the <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>payments for<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> “<span lang="eng latin">harms and compensations</span>” <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sukha-duḥkha</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, such as:<note n="4"><para lang="eng latin">I adopt the interpretation of the term <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sukha-duḥkha</span><!--space--> itself, and of the meanings of the items in the following list, from work in progress by Timothy Lubin.</para></note></para><dlist><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mayaṅ tan pavvah</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">areca flower that does not bear fruit</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">valū rumambat riṅ natar</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">gourd that grows along the ground</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vipati</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">fatal accident</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vaṅkay kābunan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">bedewed corpse</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rāh kasavur iṅ dalan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">blood scattered on the street</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vākcapala</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">verbal assault</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hastacapala</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">physical assault</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dūhilatan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">accusing</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hidu kasirat</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">spitting</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mamijillākan turahniṅ kikir</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">producing the trace of a file</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mamūk</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">running amok</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mamumpaṅ</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">rape</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">lūdan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">?</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tūtan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">?</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍaṇḍa kuḍaṇḍa</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">physical abuse</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">bhaṇḍihāla</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">?</para></value><key/><value><para lang="eng latin">etc.</para></value></dlist><para lang="eng latin">The <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> founder of the freehold shall be the beneficiary of all its royal revenues.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨1r16–2r1⟩</span><!--space--> As for those who</para><dlist><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mañambul</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">paint black</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mañavriṅ</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make muslin</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅlākha</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">dye red</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅapus</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">twist ropes</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅubar</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">dye red</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">matarub</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make sheds</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅgula</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make sugar</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅdyūn</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make pots</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">manūla vuṅkudu</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">stake <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mengkudu</span><!--space--> root</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅluruṅ</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make castor-oil</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mapahaṅan</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">dye with <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pahaṅan</span></para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">magavai ruṅki, payuṅ vəlū</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make baskets, round (?) parasols</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mopih</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">work <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">upih</span></para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">akajaṅ</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make awnings</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">akisī</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make basket works</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mamubut</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">produce turnery</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅanam-anam</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">make plait</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">manavaṅ</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">catch in nets</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">manahəb mamisaṇḍuṅ manuk</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">catch or snare birds</para></value><key><para><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">makala-kalā</span></para></key><value><para lang="eng latin">?</para></value></dlist><para lang="eng latin">lOnly the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> founder of the freehold herself shall have authority over all its royal revenues.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r1–2r7⟩</span><!--space--> Likewise the numbers of those who engage in trade while present there are restricted: those not to be subjected to the collectors of royal revenue are four tradesmen of the same trade per freehold. If they buy buffaloes, the quota of buffaloes is 20 per tradesman; 40 cows per tradesman; 80 sheep per tradesman; one coop of ducks per tradesman; silver-smiths, four masters; wagoners, four yokes; drum <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>players<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span>, three places to perform; carpenters, two tradesman; goldsmiths, two smithies; coppersmiths, two smithies; ironsmiths, two smithies; <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">cadar</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>cloth<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> makers, eight <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">cadar</span><!--space--> making places. If their wares are carried by shoulder-pole — such as cloth vendors, tinkers <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>vendors of copper utensils<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, condiment sellers, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kacapuri</span><!--space--> makers, transporters of cotton, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mengkudu</span>, iron, copper, bronze, tin, block salt, sesame, uncooked rice, sugar, safflower <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>seeds?<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> — all types of wares that are carried by shoulder-pole: altogether there may be five shoulder-pole bundles per tradesman. What is up to that limit will not be subjected to the collectors of royal revenue, wherever <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>those traders<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> might go, whatever may be their country <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of origin<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>. However, they should keep the present document. If <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>their trade<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> is more than the limit <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>imposed<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> on them, they will be subjected <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>to levies<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> by the collectors of all royal shares <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sa-uddhāra haji</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> proportionally to the excess <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>but taxation of the excess<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> should not be disproportional <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tan adhikāna</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>.<note n="5"><para lang="eng latin">The form <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">adhikāna</span><!--space--> is apparently an irrealis passive from *<span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">umadhike</span><!--space--> ‘to exceed’; it seems that Zoetmulder (<span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Zoetmulder 1982">1982</span></link>, p. s.vv. adhikāra I.2 and tan adhikara</span>) has not precisely understood the expression <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tan adhikāna</span>.</para></note></para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨2r7–3r14⟩</span><!--space--> At that time the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān kabayān</span>, mother of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> minister of Halu, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Sahasra, offered gifts:</para><elist type="plain"><item><para lang="eng latin">to the Great King: 5 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan gañjar haji</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>royal reward cloth<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">to the Hino: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>s, 8 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan jaro haji</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>to<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> minister of Halu, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Sahasra: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> Vka, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Bālīśvara: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> Sirikan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Balyaṅ: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the official of Kanuruhan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Udā: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Tiruan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍapunta</span><!--space--> Taritip: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Vadihati, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Dinakara: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Akudur, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Tunaṅ: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Maṅhuri, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Paṇḍamuan: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Halaran, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Bhā: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Paku Bāṅ, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ra</span><!--space--> Limus: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Vadiṅin, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Tīkṣṇa: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the 2 masters of Vadihati, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>namely<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> the Mira-Mirah, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Sudānta <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the Halaran, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Ləbur Poh: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the 2 masters of Makudur, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>namely<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>Təpusən <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Ulahan: 5 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Paṅuraṅ of Vadihati, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ ra</span><!--space--> Vuṅū, watching over <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Deva: 5 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Paṅuraṅ of Makudur, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ ra</span><!--space--> Kəvəl, watching over <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Baruṇa: 5 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headmanship of Vadihati, old and young: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headmanship of Makudur, old and young: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the holy Kudur: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 set of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">all the masters of the Secretariat <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pakaraṇān</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>:<note n="6"><para lang="eng latin">Regarding the term <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pakaraṇān</span>, see our commentary on the <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKWuruduKidul" lang="eng latin">Wurudu Kidul</link><!--space--> inscription.</para></note></para><elist type="plain"><item><para lang="eng latin">the supervisor of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">nāyaka</span>-ship of the Great King, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Kuṇḍū</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the supervisor of junior troops, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Siriṅ</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the supervisor of servants, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Tapa</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Kaṇḍamuhi, master Nārāyaṇa</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the calligrapher, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Nidhi;</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the herald of Halu Visāga, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍapunta</span><!--space--> Basu; of Vka Viridih, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍapunta</span><!--space--> Niścaya; of Paṅkaja: <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍapunta</span><!--space--> Govinda; of Kanuruhan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ ra</span><!--space--> Mase; of Tiruan Sumuḍan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Brahmaśiva</para></item></elist><para lang="eng latin">collectively 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s.</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">calligraphers to the Great King:</para><elist type="plain"><item><para lang="eng latin">Lakṣaṇa</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Baḍiva</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Kīrti</para></item></elist><para lang="eng latin">4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">both <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih juru</span>s, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vaśah</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Śabda, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuci</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Makāra: 5 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the heralds of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih vaśah</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and of<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">piṅsor hyaṅ</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Pakudān: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kupaṅ</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the heralds of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih kuci</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and of<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the pasəkaran <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Caitya: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kupaṅ</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> of Bavaṅ Mapapan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Gula: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>s, 8 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 set of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the foreman of Bavaṅ, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Malva <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the guardian of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> of Bavaṅ, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Bukəṅ: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān vulat patih</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Ḍohol, was given 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan jaro haji</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the official of Sijana, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Pujut <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>was given<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan sə̄ṅ rāh</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>“<span lang="eng latin">Sparkling Blood Cloth</span>”<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>one of<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Təasan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>i.e., Tyasan<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Toloboṅ, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>who was<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> also <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">atəhər</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span><!--space--> ready to carry out the clearing <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of wood and underbrush<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> at Bavaṅ, was given as gifts: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the official of Muṅgaṅ, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Maṅubər, village of Muṅgaṅ, foreman of the group of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">nāyaka</span>-ship at Halu: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kakaran</span>, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pu</span><!--space--> Munda: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Ghoṣa, teaming up with <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>? <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">amasaṅakan i</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><note n="7"><para lang="eng latin">The term <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">amasaṅakan i</span>, apparently not recorded by <!--space--><span><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link></span>, is found also in the <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKPalepangan" lang="eng latin">Palepangan</link><!--space--> inscription (1v12).</para></note><!--space--> the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> of Bavaṅ Mapapan, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Gula: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Pataləsan, district of Halu, village of Tumurun: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Jula, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">partaya</span><!--space--> of Air Banua, village of Virikan: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Praśānta, foreman of servants at Kanuruhan, village of Taṅunan: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>s, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Bajra <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Kumo: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>, ready to carry out the clearing <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of wood and underbrush<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> at Paḍaṅ <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Paṅkaja, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Baruṇa: 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span><!--space--> of Ləmah Tulis, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Kilipan: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Tumpuk: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Dəan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>i.e., Dyan<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> Kāma, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span><!--space--> of Paḍaṅ: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Rāvaṇa: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Bikər, Hujuṅ, Juṅhur, Dəmə, Gacəm: 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kupaṅ</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the helper of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span><!--space--> of Ləmah Tulis, ready to carry out the clearing <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of wood and underbrush<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">Candra, Bhanū, Bakaṭəl, Ḍaluṅ: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span><!--space--> of Bavaṅ <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Manujendra: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">his two children were each given 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kupaṅ</span>s</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s of Mapapan, ready to carry out the clearing <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of wood and underbrush<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Caitra, Urak <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Arja: collectively 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s; indvidually, 1 set of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>;</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s of Mapapan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Bandhu, father of Rakā, Burəṅ, Kuñjar, Paradhīpa, Baliba, Śrayisinta, Tahimbək; the heralds: Jaluk, Sūṅan, Kulumā, Baracah, Maṅḍampak; the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vinəkas</span>es: Surupī, Dhara: collectively 7 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>s; individually 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span><!--space--> of Pagəruyuṅ, ready to carry out the clearing <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of wood and underbrush<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Bayad: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span><!--space--> of Vəsi-vəsi, ready to carry out the clearing <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>of wood and underbrush<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Baiśākha: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span>, 4 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>;</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headmen <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rāma</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> of Ālasantan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>namely<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Dr̥vyak, Rambay, Sukhamī <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Cleṅ were given 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suvarṇa</span><!--space--> collectively</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headmen of Ləmah Tulis, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>namely<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the venerable Śikha, Pahəh, Dhaneṣṭi, Kuludū <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Bandī, were also given 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headman of Səkar Bila <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Pəgeṅ <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the headman of Kabayān Maṅambah: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headman of Ləbuh <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Runtiṅ, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kalaṅ</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Susup: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headman of Paḍaṅan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Vudyaṅ <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the supervisor of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kalaṅ</span>s <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Karuk: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headman of Tirim <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Paṇḍai <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the supervisor of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kalaṅ</span>s <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Manah: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headman of Lāpan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Rūpa <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> the supervisor of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kalaṅ</span>s <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Kūrma: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, per person</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the headman of Vulu Taji <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Kurul: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Vila at Ālasantan, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">si</span><!--space--> Piluk: 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Paṅuraṅ <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Balun: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Kriṅ <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Landəp: 1 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Makudur who will move forward to demarcate, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Ḍaṇḍər: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 set of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, together with gifts, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>i.e.<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> equiped with offerings</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the Vadihati who will move forward to demarcate, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> Harava: 10 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 set of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>, together with gifts, <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>i.e.<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> equiped with offerings</para></item></elist><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r14–3r17⟩</span><!--space--> After giving the gifts and the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span><!--space--> to all of them, the offerings for the Makudur were placed below the canopy. The one serving as mendicant poured water to purify the “<span lang="eng latin">heart</span>” for piercing and the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kulumpaṅ</span><!--space--> stone. The Makudur arose to offer salutation to the holy “<span lang="eng latin">heart</span>” and sat down below the canopy, having his feet as support <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>i.e., he knelt<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, facing the holy “<span lang="eng latin">heart</span>”, wearing as <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">siṅhəl</span><!--space--> 1 pair of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>. The Vadihati followed. The Makudur began to <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">uyut</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> cleaved the neck of the chicken, using the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kulumpaṅ</span>-stone as underlay, and bashed an egg on the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma</span><!--space--> stone, pronouncing an admonition and swearing an oath to all those whom they traditionally admonish so that the holy freehold will be respected:</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨3r17–4r10⟩</span><!--space--> “<span lang="eng latin">Pay heed you gods at Śrī Haricandana, Great Seer Agasti, East, South, West, North, Center, Zenith, Nadir Sun, Moon, Earth, Water, Wind, Fire, Yajamāna, Ether Dharma, Day and Night, Twilight, Heart Yakṣas, Rākṣasas, Piśācas, Pretas, Asuras, Garuḍa, Gandharvas Four Lokapālas: Yama, Varuṇa, Kubera, Vāsava <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>i.e., Indra<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> And sons of the gods: Five Kuśikas, Nandīśvara, Mahākāla, Six Vināyakas, Snake-Kings, goddess Durgā Quadrangular <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>Aśvins?<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, Endless <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>Ananta<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, Surendra children of the gods Kāla and Mr̥tyu: Gaṇas and Bhūtas you well-known ones who protect the palace of the Great King at Mḍaṅ in the land of Matarām you who take part in embodying, entering in all bodies you all who as witnesses watch far and near, by day and by night, while you listen to the pledge, the sworn oath <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">śapatha sumpah</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, the admonition of us to all you gods! If the bad man does not hold firm, does not keep that oath being submitted by the Vahuta Hyaṅ Kudur, lord and servant, old and young, man and female, mendicant and householder, as well as <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>s, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s and headmen — whoever disturbs the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">varuk</span><!--space--> land at Ālasantan as a <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> freehold granted by the Great King to the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān kabayān</span>, mother of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> Minister of Halu <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>called<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dyah</span><!--space--> Sahasra, up to the future’s future may he split asunder! All the more so if he shall uproot the holy <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīma</span><!--space--> stone. Therefore, even if he must be killed by you, o gods, your approach when you kill him should be without looking back, not looking to the side, clash with the opponent, slap his left side, attack anew his right side, cut down his snout, split his skull, rip open his belly, stretch out his intestines, draw out his entrails, tear out his liver, without delay finish off, bring to an end his life. If he goes to the forest, may he be eaten up by the tiger, bitten by the snake, whirled round and round by divine wrath! If he goes to the field, may he be caught by thunderclap, may his head be torn off by demons, may he be eaten by the Pamuṅvan giants! Unceasingly in his home all diseases torment him! Please you gods Kuśika, Garga, Metrī, Kuruṣya and Pātañjala, guardian of the North, guardian of the South, of the West, of the East, throw <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>him<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> in the air, tear <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>him<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> asunder (?) among all gods, throw <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>him<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> into the great ocean, drown <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>him<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> at the dam! May he be caught by the holy gods in the water! May he be pulled by sea-monsters! May he be snatched by the crocodile! May it be there that he dies, the lawless man who dissolves the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> freehold at Ālasantan. May he suffer misfortune among all ancestors <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">devata</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>! May he go to ruins, may be engulfed in the Phīra! May he reach the hells! May he be dropped in the Great Raurava! May he be cooked by the forces of Yama! May he be hammered no less than seven times by the Kiṅkaras <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>servants of Yama<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>! When he is given shape, it will only be a bad one. He will suffer as long as he lives! May all types of pain be experienced by him! May all sorts of pain be undergone by him! Every detestable thing among born creatures is what he will become! May he rot without finding rest! Leave behind fanned ash! Thus will become the lawless man who dissolves the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> freehold at Ālasantan!</span>”</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r11–4r20⟩</span><!--space--> After the Makudur had performed <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">uyut</span>, they all changed clothes. They all sat in the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tkan pasək</span>,<note n="8"><para lang="eng latin"><span><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link></span><!--space--> only records <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pəkan pasək</span><!--space--> “<span lang="eng latin">a square near the palace (where those who offer pasĕk assemble)</span>”, but the inscriptions of this period consistently present what seems to be the same term in the form <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tkan pasək</span>.</para></note><!--space--> following the order of those who are present in the capacity<note n="9"><para lang="eng latin">The expression <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ hana riṅ kon</span><!--space--> might be an equivalent to the expression <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">maṅagam kon</span><!--space--> commonly used in earlier periods to express a meaning like “<span lang="eng latin">encumbent, on duty</span>”, but I tentatively opt for a word-for-word interpretation “<span lang="eng latin">to be in the capacity</span>”.</para></note><!--space--> of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>, headman of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kabayān</span><!--space--> as well as headman of neighboring villages, temple managers <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kulapati</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">punta</span>s, seniors, juniors, men, women, inferior, intermediate and superior. No one was left behind in taking part in feasting <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">manaḍah</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> on the market place of Ālasantan:</para><elist type="plain"><item><para lang="eng latin">being regaled,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">being offered dressed-up rice <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>? <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">skul dāndānan</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">being ladled <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hinirusan</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>:<note n="10"><para lang="eng latin"><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.v. irusan</span><!--space--> is in need of correction, as it is based on a single passage from a reissued inscription. An entry needs to be added for the verbal derivation <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">aṅirusi</span><!--space--> of which <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hinirusan</span><!--space--> is the passive form.</para></note></para><elist type="bulleted"><item><para lang="eng latin">cooked foods <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kəla-kəla</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">snacks <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ambil-ambil</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kasyan</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">lit-lit</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tlu saraṇak</span>,<note n="11"><para lang="eng latin"><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.v. ranak II</span><!--space--> is in need of correction, as it is based on only two epigraphic passages that have not been reliably edited. Multiple inscriptions of this period read <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tlu saraṇak/saranak</span>. Since the meaning of the second word is not transparent, the meaning of the expression as a whole isn’t clear, but it seems to be literally “<span lang="eng latin">three per <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ranak</span></span>”.</para></note></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">roasted food <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅa-saṅān</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">banana core <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">haryas</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rumba-rumbah</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuluban</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tetis</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tumpuk-tumpuk</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">unsalted dried meat <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍeṁ hañaṅ</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">salted dried meat <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḍeṁ hasin</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">cockup <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>fish<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">shrimps <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">huraṅ</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">bijañjan</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>fish<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,<note n="12"><para lang="eng latin">This <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hapax legomenon</span><!--space--> could be emended to <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">biluṁluṁ jañjan</span>, but <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vijañjan</span><!--space--> may be attested in <!--space--><span tip="Work title"><span class="title" lang="eng latin">Bhomāntaka</span></span><!--space--> 81.42 (depending on how the words are divided). See <!--space--><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.v. kaḍavi and jañjan</span>.</para></note></para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kaḍivas</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>fish<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kavan</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>fish<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">laya-layar</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin"><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">hala-hala</span>,</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">eggs</para></item></elist></item><item><para lang="eng latin">being relaxed with liquor,<note n="13"><para lang="eng latin">The expression <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">inariṅ suṇḍa</span><!--space--> has never been understood by previous scholars, and figures in some more or less confusingly misread forms in existing editions (e.g., <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">inarisuṇḍa</span>). It contains <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">suṇḍa</span><!--space--> that must be a loanword from Sanskrit <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">śuṇḍā</span>, not recorded in <!--space--><span><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link></span>.</para></note><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">atak</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> milk</para></item></elist><para lang="eng latin">The total of containers <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">limas</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> per <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>banana<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> leaf was nine, apart from the fish.<note n="14"><para lang="eng latin">The arrangement described here seems reminiscent of the South Indian <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">thali</span>.</para></note><!--space--> The number of buffaloes that were eaten is 3. The one was eaten agonistically <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>? <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">praṅ paṅpaṅ</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> by the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">taṇḍa rakryān</span><!--space--> and the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>s, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s and headmen. The 2 were eaten collectively by the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">taṇḍa rakryān</span><!--space--> and the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>s, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s and headmen. 3 deer, 1 boar, 1 goose were eaten collectively. They drank <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">sīdhu</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuak</span>, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kilaṅ</span><!--space--> three times each. They were fortified with <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tambul</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>“<span lang="eng latin">refreshments</span>”<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><note n="15"><para lang="eng latin">It seems rather clear, from comparison with line 4r17, that <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vinuvuhan</span><!--space--> must here imply the group of people as subject, and that the gloss “<span lang="eng latin">to increase, add to</span>” in <!--space--><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.vv. amuvuhi, vinuvuhan, kawuwuhan</span><!--space--> is inadequate.</para></note><!--space--> and given <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuravu</span>, dates, tamarind <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">dodol</span><!--space--> as desert <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>? <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">inañjapan</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>.<note n="16"><para lang="eng latin">The phrase <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">inañjapan kuravu kurima asam dvadval</span><!--space--> occurs in a few contemporary inscriptions (e.g. <!--space--><link href="/texts/INSIDENKLinggasuntan" lang="eng latin">Linggasuntan</link><!--space--> b27–28). <!--space--><span><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link></span><!--space--> records only <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuravuṅ</span>, but the final nasal is based on only one occurrence, in the <!--space--><span tip="Work title"><span class="title" lang="eng latin">Kakavin Rāmāyaṇa</span></span>, while none of the inscriptions ever shows a clear <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">anusvāra</span>, so I propose to accept <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuravu</span>. About this word, Tom Hoogervorst wrote to me (email 29/08/2020): “<span lang="eng latin">I have wanted to connect Old Javanese <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuravuṅ</span><!--space--> with modern Javanese <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">krawu</span><!--space--> and Malay <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kerabu</span><!--space--> – a dish consisting of mixed condiments whose precise ingredients differ regionally – in the paper co-authored with Jiří, but decided against it because I couldn’t explain the word-final nasal. Now that it should apparently be <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kuravu</span>, my theory “works” after all.</span>” Regarding <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kurima</span>, from the same email: “<span lang="eng latin">In Malay, <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kurma</span><!--space--> has two meanings: 1) a date (the fruit) from Persian <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">ḵẖurmā</span>, and 2) a meat stew from Persian <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">qorma</span>. The second meaning can be excluded in this context, given the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">pencuci mulut</span><!--space--> nature of the surrounding words. I’ve only managed to find <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kurima</span><!--space--> in the <!--space--><span tip="Work title"><span class="title" lang="eng latin">Bausastra: Jarwa Kawi</span></span>, Padmasusastra, 1903 (https://www.sastra.org/bahasa-dan-budaya/kamus-dan-leksikon/198-bausastra-jarwa-kawi-padmasusastra-1903-11-bagian-1-ha-ka), which lists it as a synonym for ‘mango’. This probably means that the word at one point denoted a specific variety of said fruit. If we assume a Persian etymology, I would have no problems with the time depth, nor with a possible semantic shift to ‘mango’ (cf. <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mangga apulkat</span><!--space--> and <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mangga apel</span><!--space--> in more recent times). The only thing I couldn’t help you with is the interconsonantal /i/. Javanese has no problem with the segment /rm/, so my best guess would be borrowing through some South Asian vernacular.</span>” The base <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">añjap</span><!--space--> is not recorded in dictionaries.</para></note><!--space--> Side by side, they took drinks,<note n="17"><para lang="eng latin">It seems unikely that <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">malarih</span><!--space--> here means “<span lang="eng latin">server</span>”, as per <!--space--><span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.v. alarih</span><!--space--> “<span lang="eng latin">server (of drinks)</span>”, and more likely that it means “<span lang="eng latin">to take drinks</span>” (<span lang="study_other latin"><link href="/bibliography/entry/Zoetmulder1982_01"><span lang="study_other latin">Zoetmulder 1982</span></link>, s.v. larih</span><!--space--> “<span lang="eng latin">drinks, refreshment</span>”).</para></note><!--space--> while offering unguents <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> flowers <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>? <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">jənu səkar</span><display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span>. The <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuvuṅ</span><!--space--> player played. The Lord of Vulat Patih, the official of Sijana, the foremen of Halu, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">patih</span>s, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vahuta</span>s, the headmen rose to pay homage facing straight in the direction of the Great King and of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān mapatih</span>, prior to dancing. They danced to the crooked <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tuvuṅ</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> to the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">gaṇḍiṅ</span>. After dancing, they sat down again. They were fortified with <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tambul</span><!--space--> and given drinks a second time each. They offered <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">baṅlus</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Text inserted into the translation as explanation or disambiguation" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">(</display>fish<display lang="study_other latin">)</display></span><!--space--> while being given drinks again. Then they staged a cock-fight. They hired</para><elist type="plain"><item><para lang="eng latin">the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">tapuk</span><!--space--> performer called Kampəlan who was given 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the jester called Paṅin-Aṅan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>who was given<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>; <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>and<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> <span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">si</span><!--space--> Taratakan <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>who was given<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">kupaṅ</span>s</para></item><item><para lang="eng latin">the actor called Vāhu who was given 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span></para></item></elist><para lang="eng latin">After watching the performances, they all went home. At night, the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān</span><!--space--> <span class="supplied" tip="Editorial addition to clarify interpretation"><display lang="study_other latin">⟨</display><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">mapatih</span><!--space--> <display lang="study_other latin">⟩</display></span><!--space--> hired a wayang <!--space--><span class="supplied" tip="Text added to the translation for the sake of target language syntax" lang="eng latin"><display lang="study_other latin">[</display>performer<display lang="study_other latin">]</display></span><!--space--> called <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">si</span><!--space--> Kapuluṅan who was given 2 <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">māṣa</span>s, 1 sheet of <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">vḍihan</span>.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r19–4r20⟩</span><!--space--> Such were the proceedings of the demarcation of the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">paṅurumbigyan</span><!--space--> freehold at Ālasantan by the <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">rakryān kabayān</span>. May it be known to all good people that the holy freehold is irrevocable.</para><para lang="eng latin"><span class="lb" tip="Line start" lang="eng latin">⟨4r20⟩</span><!--space--> The plates were engraved by <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="source_other source_other">saṅ</span><!--space--> Kīrti, calligrapher to the Great King.</para></translation><bibliography><head lang="eng latin">Bibliography</head><div phantom="false"><para lang="eng latin">First edited by Wibowo (<span><link href="#bib-Wibowo1979_01"><span lang="study_other latin" tip="Wibowo 1979">1979</span></link></span>). Re-edited here by Arlo Griffiths from photographs by Nigel Bullough. The rather numerous instances where Wibowo’s edition shows small errors of reading due to omission of a diacritic mark (macron or underdot) have been passed over in silence in the apparatus that accompanies the present edition, as have most variant readings that involve punctuation signs.</para></div><div phantom="false"><head lang="study_other latin">Primary</head><para class="bib-entry" anchor="bib-Wibowo1979_01" lang="eng latin"><span class="bold" lang="study_other latin">[W]</span><!--space--> Wibowo, A. S. 1979. “Prasasti Ālasantan tahun 861 Śaka.” <!--space--><span class="italics journal-abbr" tip="&lt;span class=&quot;italics&quot; xml:lang=&quot;study_other latin&quot;&gt;Majalah Arkeologi&lt;/span&gt;" lang="study_other latin">MA</span><!--space--> 2 (3), pp. 3–51.</para></div><div phantom="false"><head lang="study_other latin">Secondary</head><para class="bib-entry" anchor="bib-NurhadiMagetsari+al1979_01" lang="eng latin">Nurhadi Magetsari, A.S. Wibowo, Hasan Djafar, I Gusti Agung Ayu Ratnadi, Ayu Ratnadi, Siti Kusparyanti Budhiono, Sumarti Nurhadi and Ayatrohaedi. 1979. <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="study_other latin">Kamus Arkeologi Indonesia 2</span>. Jakarta: Proyek Penelitian Bahasa dan Sastra Indonesia dan Daerah, Departemen Pendidikan dan Kebudayaan. Page 6.</para><para class="bib-entry" anchor="bib-RitaFitriati1990_01" lang="eng latin">Rita Fitriati. 1990. “Pasak-Pasak dari Prasasti Masa Balitung dan Siṇḍok.” In: <!--space--><span class="italics" lang="study_other latin">Monumen: karya persembahan untuk Prof. Dr. R. Soekmono</span>. Edited by Edi Sedyawati, Ingrid H.E. Pojoh and Supratikno Rahardjo. Seri Penerbitan Ilmiah 11 (Edisi Khusus). Depok: Lembaran Sastra, Fakultas Sastra Universitas Indonesia, pp. 102–124. Pages 106, 110–111, 124.</para><para class="bib-entry" anchor="bib-HadiSidomulyo2011_01" lang="eng latin">Hadi Sidomulyo. 2011. “Kṛtanagara and the Resurrection of Mpu Bharāda.” <!--space--><span class="italics journal-abbr" tip="&lt;span class=&quot;italics&quot; xml:lang=&quot;study_other latin&quot;&gt;Indonesia and the Malay World&lt;/span&gt;" lang="study_other latin">IMW</span><!--space--> 39 (113), pp. 123–142. DOI: <!--space--><span class="url"><link href="https://doi.org/10.1080/13639811.2010.513882" lang="study_other latin">10.1080/13639811.2010.513882</link></span>. <!--space--><link href="http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/ref/10.1080/13639811.2010.513882" lang="study_other latin">[URL]</link>. Pages 124, 135, 137.</para><para class="hanging"><span class="invalid" tip="Not in bibliography" lang="eng latin">AndiMuhammadSaid+al018_01</span></para></div></bibliography></document>